      "rev_n_1_3de02f_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_3de02f_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_3de02f",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through every cell in the grid. For each cell, check if it starts a valid horizontal word (consecutive non-# characters) or a vertical word. Extract all such words with length >= 2, store them in a list, and finally sort the list lexicographically to find the smallest one.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508380.9869523
      },
      "rev_n_2_7082d5_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_7082d5_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_7082d5",
        "thought_text": "Use a two-pass approach: first pass scans all rows to extract horizontal words, second pass scans all columns to extract vertical words. Combine both sets of words, filter for length >= 2, and use Python's built-in min() function with a custom key or direct string comparison to find the lexicographically smallest word efficiently.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508380.9869783
      },
      "rev_n_3_4e5942_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_4e5942_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_4e5942",
        "thought_text": "Since the grid size is small (up to 20x20), a brute-force approach is feasible. Generate all possible substrings from horizontal and vertical sequences of letters, validate their lengths, and maintain a running minimum string variable that updates whenever a lexicographically smaller valid word is found during the scan.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508380.9869866
      },
      "rev_n_4_7e90d6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_7e90d6_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_7e90d6",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through every cell in the grid. For each cell, attempt to build a horizontal word by moving right until a block or grid edge is hit, and a vertical word by moving down. Store all valid words with length >= 2 in a list, then sort the list lexicographically and pick the first one.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4284364204132802724",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508409.9263687
      },
      "rev_n_5_1aa172_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_1aa172_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_1aa172",
        "thought_text": "Initialize a set to store unique words. Scan each row to extract contiguous sequences of letters; if a sequence length is >= 2, add it to the set. Repeat for each column. Finally, convert the set to a sorted list and select the minimum element.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4284364204132802724",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508409.9263844
      },
      "rev_n_6_8de0b6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_8de0b6_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_8de0b6",
        "thought_text": "Since the grid size is small (up to 20x20), efficiency is not a primary concern. A straightforward brute-force approach that collects all candidate words from rows and columns, filters by length, and uses Python's built-in min() function with the default string comparison will be sufficient and easiest to implement correctly.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4284364204132802724",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508409.9263933
      },
      "rev_n_5_1aa172_1": {
